{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794739048,"nonce":"tQ6auYnt7wjCdOlP+YJB3DtKnz4=","title":"hxn-gpu: a universal GPU API over the driver libraries, with its core in Haxe","message":"The API a Haxe program uses on any GPU, shaped like WebGPU: devices, buffers, textures, bind groups by (group, binding) as hxnsl's LAYOUT describes them, pipelines, and command encoders, over the driver libraries hxn-gpu-metal (12f36ff10d50d13130455e2aee5f7d0be389989f36d6615917defad2c97ba917), hxn-gpu-vulkan (3338cab), hxn-gpu-d3d12 (c879e5b), hxn-gpu-gles (4302b3a), hxn-gpu-webgpu (5b1345d) and hxn-gpu-webgl2 (6cd5e17).\n\n\n- Synchronization is implicit in hxn-gpu, as in WebGPU: a program declares how it uses each resource, and hxn-gpu inserts barriers and layout transitions. WebGPU, WebGL2, GLES and Metal (with hazard tracking on) already work that way.\n- The drivers expose barriers explicitly, in the form Vulkan's synchronization2 and D3D12's enhanced barriers share (stages, access and layout, before and after), and do no tracking. One tracker in hxn-gpu emits them, so behaviour is the same on every backend and tested in one place, and a program using a driver directly pays for no tracking it does not want. Metal needs none while its hazard tracking is on.\n- The core is Haxe: API, validation, bind group resolution against hxnsl's tables, resource-state tracking and the barrier decisions, so Haxe developers can contribute most of it and test it with `hxn check`. hxn 0.3.2's `sources/` lets it ship as hand-written Haxe in an hxn package. Each driver's Haxe binding is what it calls.\n- Asynchronous results (mapping a buffer, the GPU finishing work) are hxn futures, settled from Metal's completion handlers and from Vulkan and D3D12 fences.\n- First version: one queue; whole buffers and texture subresources tracked; WebGPU's rule against reading and writing a resource in one pass checked.\n\n\nMeasured on 2026-11-15 on an Apple-silicon Mac with its own GPU, Rust in release, one run per runtime (Ash's tiered mode three times), median of 7 samples. Benchmark source: on 840e83e, the issue on handle cost.\n\nEncoding one Metal draw (a 16-byte setVertexBytes and a one-point draw), ns per draw, 10,000 draws in one pass:\n\n| Runtime | Two calls per draw | One call per draw | Haxe stream, one replay call | Rust alone |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ash, tiered | 59.8-64.3 | 56.2-60.9 | 51.8-54.1 | 50.9-53.2 |\n| Ash, native build | 60.0 | 54.7 | 52.3 | 50.0 |\n| hxcpp | 62.9 | 56.5 | 56.0 | 51.6 |\n| CPPIA | 100.6 | 89.6 | 81.0 | 50.5 |\n| Ash, JIT only | 60.1 | 55.7 | 51.1 | 50.6 |\n| Ash, interpreter | 419.8 | 265.1 | 205.0 | 49.6 |\n\nOn the compiled runtimes, two calls per draw cost about 10 ns over Metal's own 50: about 0.1 ms per 10,000 draws. So a Haxe core calling the driver once per command is affordable there. Under CPPIA the Haxe side adds 30-50 ns per draw; recording commands into a `Bytes` stream that one call replays recovers most of it. Plan the stream as an optimization for CPPIA and heavy frames, not as the design: a versioned command format between the Haxe core and each driver, with a `submit(stream)` entry point in the driver. Instancing, indirect draws and argument buffers lower the call count on every runtime and belong in the API either way.\n\nNot measured: stock HashLink (its local command does not work on Apple silicon), Windows, Linux, other GPUs, and Vulkan or D3D12 encoding costs.","files":null}]}